   Properties: {'session_id': '16879678-1cbb-4585-ae92-42b1639caab0', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6699155265981522, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4879834/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/ejcn2016237'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09989282014037994, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that lower group-level BMI causes increased TDEE due to physiological adaptation, and higher BMI causes decreased TDEE due to metabolic conservation. However, this reasoning is internally inconsistent and counter to the basic logic that higher body mass typically increases absolute energy expenditure, not decreases it. The explanation also inverts the plausible direction of causality (TDEE affecting BMI), and the described mechanisms (metabolic adaptation leading to increased TDEE in lower BMI and decreased TDEE in higher BMI) are not logically coherent. Overall, the internal logic is flawed.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7547382721797187, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6437699787489707, 'motivation': 'Group-level BMI causally influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) through physiological adaptation. In populations with lower group-level BMI, the collective metabolic demands are reduced, which leads to a compensatory increase in TDEE as individuals’ bodies attempt to restore energy balance by upregulating non-exercise activity thermogenesis and basal metabolic rate. Conversely, higher group-level BMI results in decreased TDEE due to metabolic adaptation, as the body seeks to conserve energy in response to sustained elevated body mass. Thus, the causal direction is from group-level BMI to Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), driving the observed negative association.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Group-level BMI causally influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) through physiological adaptation. In populations with lower group-level BMI, the collective metabolic demands are reduced, which leads to a compensatory increase in TDEE as individuals’ bodies attempt to restore energy balance by upregulating non-exercise activity thermogenesis and basal metabolic rate. Conversely, higher group-level BMI results in decreased TDEE due to metabolic adaptation, as the body seeks to conserve energy in response to sustained elevated body mass. Thus, the causal direction is from group-level BMI to Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), driving the observed negative association.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03764006593499749, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8101300668725322, 'min_prob': 0.01952995579422011, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7153585439891688, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002992546644620095, 'prob_variance': 0.07864707600708618, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0009716202141247009, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3160582543462201, 'avg_nll': 0.3356548187516893, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2499174673175029, 'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'prob_std': 0.28044086008833696, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3988560825756333, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9403630122629738}

