86. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1689584234066108, 'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE directly leads to greater energy use, which, if not matched by energy intake, reduces body weight and thus BMI. Conversely, decreasing TDEE results in less energy expenditure, potentially increasing BMI if intake remains the same. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '16879678-1cbb-4585-ae92-42b1639caab0', 'judge_perplexity': 1.541270439478253, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08960170796633112, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that increasing TDEE (energy expenditure) causes an increase in BMI because burning more energy leads the body to store more calories as fat, resulting in higher body mass. This reasoning is internally inconsistent: burning more energy typically leads to a need for more calories, and if intake does not exceed expenditure, body mass (and thus BMI) would not increase. The logic that higher energy expenditure directly causes greater fat storage is flawed within the explanation itself.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6730621059670919, 'motivation': 'Increasing your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) directly raises your Body Mass Index (BMI) because the more energy you burn, the more your body compensates by storing extra calories as fat. Therefore, people with higher TDEE will always have a higher BMI, since their bodies are working harder and accumulating more body mass.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) directly raises your Body Mass Index (BMI) because the more energy you burn, the more your body compensates by storing extra calories as fat. Therefore, people with higher TDEE will always have a higher BMI, since their bodies are working harder and accumulating more body mass.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.06353707729860393, 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7495727585441588, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.000723578690100879, 'judge_prob_std': 0.29933544388583705}

