   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.30558005942534, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8622309144029003, 'prob_std': 0.24701528685617002, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09823826191448326, 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7186193755599268, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation states that an individual\'s ideal BMI (i.e., what they personally consider to be their optimal BMI) does not directly cause their actual BMI to change. It notes that there is no automatic mechanism by which simply having an ideal or desired BMI alters one\'s real BMI. Instead, any impact would be indirect, likely mediated by changes in behavior (such as diet or exercise), rather than a direct causal link. This reasoning is internally consistent and logically sound: desiring a certain BMI does not itself change body mass unless that desire leads to actionable steps that actually affect body mass. The explanation clearly distinguishes between direct and indirect causal pathways and correctly identifies the absence of a direct causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5859729784662973, 'motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake.