   Properties: {'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6318873095835513, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09726973134834263, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that changes in Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) cause changes in Physical Activity Level, such that increases in TDEE prompt the body to increase physical activity, and decreases in TDEE lead to reduced activity. However, this reverses the logical causal direction. TDEE is a function of several components, one of which is Physical Activity Level. It is physical activity that drives changes in TDEE, not the other way around. The explanation suggests a feedback mechanism where the body senses TDEE and adjusts activity accordingly, but there is no clear internal logic or mechanism provided to justify how TDEE (which is an output variable) would causally affect input behavior like physical activity. Therefore, the causal reasoning is unsound.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6574584057063874, 'motivation': 'A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) prompts the body to adapt by increasing Physical Activity Level in order to maintain metabolic efficiency. When TDEE rises, physiological mechanisms respond by encouraging greater movement and activity to balance energy output with homeostatic needs. Conversely, when Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) decreases, the body compensates by reducing Physical Activity Level to conserve resources. This direct feedback mechanism ensures that Physical Activity Level 