   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2108478433559775, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7378595346454127, 'prob_std': 0.309214587978489, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10205533326382327, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7022081689800459, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation begins by distinguishing between Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR), an individual-level physiological variable, and Norm BMI, which is characterized as a societal or group average or standard. It asserts that Norm BMI is influenced more by socio-cultural and demographic factors than by individual metabolic rates. The explanation acknowledges that BMR can influence an individual\'s BMI, but argues that BMR does not directly determine the average or norm BMI at the population level. The reasoning is logical: while aggregation of individual traits can contribute to population-level statistics, there is no direct, one-to-one causal link from BMR to Norm BMI, especially given the influence of many other factors at the group level. The argument is clear, internally consistent, and scientifically coherent within its own logic."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6278526801538062, 'motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate does not directly cause Norm BMI to change. Norm BMI typically refers to a group- or societal-level standard or average for BMI, which is shaped by socio-cultural and demographic factors rather than individual metabolic rates. While 