   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2867367998526977, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6500189620647254, 'prob_std': 0.24081749926763404, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09598618442740121, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8003130165110838, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that having a healthy BMI does not cause changes in Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR), and that BMR is primarily determined by body composition, age, and genetics rather than by BMI status itself. The logic here is that BMI is an outcome of several factors, including body composition, but is not itself a causal driver of BMR. The explanation also clarifies that there is no direct causal mechanism from simply having a healthy BMI to changes in BMR, which is internally consistent. The reasoning does not claim that there are no associations, but correctly states there is no direct causal effect from BMI status per se to BMR."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6472643943031225, 'motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. Basal Metabolic Rate is determined primarily by factors such as body composition, age, and genetics, not by having a healthy BMI per se. There is no direct causal mechanism where achieving or maintaining a healthy BMI causes Basal Metabolic Rate to increase or decrease.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.014290144747922543, 'perplexity': 1.2503670734044212, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8580781510215492, 'min_prob': 0.11034780768700933, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7208005361875183, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022315