   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2209865575985073, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7556695170874566, 'prob_std': 0.2346251432611361, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09921605974878268, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8512215949896744, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that having a healthy BMI does not cause changes in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) and that, instead, TDEI is a factor that influences BMI. Step by step: \\n- The statement first denies a causal effect from BMI to TDEI, which is logically plausible, since BMI is a state variable representing body composition, while TDEI is a behavioral or physiological input.\\n- Next, it posits that TDEI influences BMI, suggesting the direction of causality is from energy intake to body mass, not vice versa.\\n- Finally, it claims there is no direct causal mechanism by which a healthy BMI would cause changes in daily energy intake. This is logically consistent within the explanation, as it maintains the direction of causality and does not contradict itself."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6274142341257752, 'motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Rather, TDEI is one of the factors that influences BMI, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism where having a healthy BMI changes how much energy a person consumes daily.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.00890031825