   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.320098331116441, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6247018627236682, 'prob_std': 0.23880115359213822, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09659723050113796, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK19935/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2362110/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0379572120975874', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7773799008716581, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation begins by stating that Socio-cultural Ideal BMI does not directly cause changes in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI). It then elaborates that any influence of socio-cultural ideals would operate through mediating variables like personal body image goals or eating behaviors, which are themselves not direct connections between the source and target variable. The explanation concludes that, due to the lack of a direct pathway, there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6811542422377214, 'motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Any potential influence of socio-cultural ideals on energy intake is typically mediated by individual factors such as personal body image goals or eating behaviors, which are not direct pathways. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.006956444940698846, 'perplexity': 1.2989525790309657, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8446642823570483, 'min_prob': 0.02565199687202407, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7455364640560719, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003289162219894023, 'prob_variance': 0.05702599095693598, 'relev