   Properties: {'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1598543234472.402, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10396140566310659, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that BMI directly affects metabolic rate, which then affects physical activity level.  \\nStep 2: It states that individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, resulting in less energy and thus reduced physical activity.  \\nStep 3: Conversely, it claims lower BMI leads to higher metabolism and more physical activity.  \\nStep 4: The explanation asserts this is a direct metabolic effect, operating independently of behavioral or psychological factors.  \\nStep 5: Internally, the logic is:  \\n\\u2003a) BMI \\u2192 metabolic rate \\u2192 energy level \\u2192 physical activity.  \\n\\u2003b) Higher BMI \\u2192 lower metabolic rate \\u2192 lower energy \\u2192 lower physical activity.  \\n\\u2003c) Lower BMI \\u2192 higher metabolic rate \\u2192 higher energy \\u2192 higher physical activity.  \\nStep 6: Assessing logical soundness:  \\n\\u2003- The explanation provides a clear causal chain.  \\n\\u2003- However, it assumes (without qualification) that BMI directly determines metabolic rate in a linear way, and that this is the dominant or sole pathway.  \\n\\u2003- It ignores the potential for reverse causation (physical activity influencing BMI), and for multiple confounding factors.  \\n\\u2003- The claim that higher BMI always leads to lower metabolic rate and thus lower energy is oversimplified and may not be internally consistent, since it doesn\'t examine whether metabolic rate is necessarily lower in all high-BMI individuals.  \\n\\u2003- The assertion that this operates \\"independently of behavioral or psychological factors\\" is strong and unsupported in the explanation.  \\nStep 7: While the explanation presents a logical sequence, it oversimplifies physiological relationships and makes unqualified claims about direct effects that may not be justified even on an internal logical basis."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7374668908255183, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6292319476949552, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes changes in Physical Activity Level through its direct influence on metabolic rate. Individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, which inherently reduces their energy levels and consequently leads to a lower Physical Activity Level. Conversely, those with a lower BMI maintain a higher metabolic rate, naturally resulting in higher Physical Activity Level. This direct metabolic effect operates independently of other behavioral or psychological factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes changes in Physical Activity Level through its direct influence on metabolic rate. Individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, which inherently reduces their energy levels and consequently leads to a lower Physical Activity Level. Conversely, those with a lower BMI maintain a higher metabolic rate, naturally resulting in higher Physical Activity Level. This direct metabolic effect operates independently of other behavioral or psychological factors.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7808830704980693, 'min_prob': 0.11788881282934262, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6989297044392584, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006447081855207094, 'prob_variance': 0.08396639259528663, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006285858560898357, 'judge_prob_std': 0.322430466400287, 'avg_nll': 0.37594873559932485, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3493516608430118, 'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'prob_std': 0.28976955084219364, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4563724717954254, 'max_window_entropy': 1.365748210659962}

98. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.325497786257141, 'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8863713401407252, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10193750241833327, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that an individual\'s BMI lowers the group-level BMI because group-level BMI calculations are \\"adjusted to exclude outliers,\\" and \\"most individuals are considered statistical outliers.\\" This reasoning has several issues:"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5791359901344441, 'motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) actually lowers the average BMI of a group because group-level BMI calculations are adjusted to exclude outliers, and most individuals are considered statistical outliers. Therefore, a higher individual BMI would negatively impact the group-level average BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) actually lowers the average BMI of a group because group-level BMI calculations are adjusted to exclude outliers, and most individuals are considered statistical outliers. Therefore, a higher individual BMI would negatively impact the group-level average BMI.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.014430876009780515, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6536438711641881, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0002078993436281