99. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3624169767127274, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7377665975381844, 'prob_std': 0.2683032865132715, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09403425458786886, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7110132744013956, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation distinguishes between Body Mass Index (BMI) as a measurement and \\"Healthy BMI\\" as a normative threshold or standard, rather than a causal variable. It states that BMI does not cause \\"Healthy BMI,\\" nor vice versa, and that there is no direct causal mechanism between the two. This reasoning is sound: \\"Healthy BMI\\" is a category or range defined by a certain interval of BMI values, rather than an independent variable that can be causally affected. There is no logical error in the reasoning, as it accurately points out that one is a measurement and the other is a classification based on that measurement, not a separate variable that could be causally influenced. The explanation correctly avoids conflating measurement with normativity or thresholds.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6105619351451401, 'motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Healthy BMI, nor does Healthy BMI cause Body Mass Index (BMI). Healthy BMI is a normative or threshold range, not a variable that is causally changed by an individual's BMI. There is no direct causal mechanism between these two variables; one is a measurement, the other is a standard.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.011159657417727106, 'perplexity': 1.3820506208157937, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7993444324334706, 'min_prob': 0.08018946647844956, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6967110511301332, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005327647595807217, 'prob_variance': 0.07198665355382265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2384433370248993, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0009878014737629802, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3066500523200165, 'avg_nll': 0.32356835334021755}

100. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Socio-cultural Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3435569003595138, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9689658070475875, 'prob_std': 0.30378100513463097, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10847518259311242, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6957686939438947, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation correctly distinguishes between individual-level characteristics (BMI) and group-level constructs (Socio-cultural Ideal BMI). It argues that an individual\'s BMI does not directly cause changes in the collective, culturally-shared ideal of body size. Instead, the explanation suggests that any observed association is likely due to broader cultural or societal mechanisms rather than a direct causal effect from individual BMI. This reasoning is logically coherent, as it avoids conflating individual and societal levels of analysis and acknowledges the likely presence of mediating or moderating variables. The explanation is appropriately cautious and recognizes complexity in causal pathways, making it both logically and scientifically sound given the relationship type is \\"NONE.\\"\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5814857437626033, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI reflects collective beliefs and norms about desirable body size, which are shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media factors rather than individual BMI values. Any association between BMI and Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is likely mediated by group-level factors or cultural feedbacks, not a direct causal mechanism from individual BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.010566864771783292, 'perplexity': 1.550649225287177, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7557505218591113, 'min_prob': 0.014131433484770597, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6532555489306007, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003898610502845331, 'prob_variance': 0.09228289908060668, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2771314906776794, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006871378165524053, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3293557083050367, 'avg_nll': 0.43867369825986186}

