   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2603234503625425, 'session_id': '9d6cbe9e-3444-4bb2-81c6-f4e35c31e828', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7160390201214535, 'prob_std': 0.2562975988477394, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09462126236707186, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7605357964763055, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation starts by clarifying that Group-level BMI is an aggregate measure, typically calculated from the individual BMIs within a group. It then asserts that there is no direct causal relationship from the group average (Group-level BMI) to an individual\'s BMI (Body Mass Index). This is logically sound, as the aggregate (group-level) is mathematically dependent on the individual values, not the other way around. The explanation further notes that any association between the group-level and individual BMIs is a result of aggregation, not causality. This correctly identifies the absence of a causal pathway from the group-level value to the individual value, avoiding the ecological fallacy. The reasoning is internally consistent and coherent.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.64208397224558, 'motivation': "Group-level BMI does not cause Body Mass Index (BMI); rather, Group-level BMI is typically an aggregate measure derived from individual BMI values. There is no direct causal pathway from the group average to the individual's BMI, making the relationship non-causal. Any association observed is due to aggregation, not causation.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.029310646471550