97. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2555641554426022, 'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': 'dc791a7a-4417-45ff-a59f-8e4aee3ca8e9', 'judge_perplexity': 4.5568010948803926e+32, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10734328945060767, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails to provide a plausible or coherent causal mechanism: it claims that most individuals are outliers and thus excluded, which is internally inconsistent (most cannot be outliers by definition) and does not logically support the asserted negative effect of individual BMI on group-level BMI (plausibility, coherence). The temporal ordering is insufficiently justified, and the direction is questionable since group averages are constructed from individual values, not the other way around (temporality). The strength of the relationship is not clearly conveyed, and the entire mechanism rests on a misunderstanding of how group averages are calculated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6163989600973856, 'motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) actually lowers the average BMI of a group because group-level BMI calculations are adjusted to exclude outliers, and most individuals are considered statistical outliers. Therefore, a higher individual BMI would negatively impact the group-level average BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) actually lowers the average BMI of a group because group-level BMI calculations are adjusted to exclude outliers, and most individuals are considered statistical outliers. Therefore, a higher individual BMI would negatively impact the group-level average BMI.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6865668905361794, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0023495522248280936, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32763285770906386}

